First, calculate the first few powers of $\mathbf{B}$:
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{B}^2 &= \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.5} \begin{pmatrix} \cos(\pi/6) & 0 & -\sin(\pi/6) \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ \sin(\pi/6) & 0 & \cos(\pi/6) \end{pmatrix} \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1} \begin{pmatrix} \cos(\pi/6) & 0 & -\sin(\pi/6) \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ \sin(\pi/6) & 0 & \cos(\pi/6) \end{pmatrix} \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} \cos(\pi/3) & 0 & -\sin(\pi/3) \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ \sin(\pi/3) & 0 & \cos(\pi/3) \end{pmatrix},
\end{align*}
where $\cos(\pi/3) = 1/2$, $\sin(\pi/3) = \sqrt{3}/2$.

Next, compute higher powers:
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{B}^3 &= \mathbf{B} \mathbf{B}^2 = \begin{pmatrix} \cos(\pi/6) & 0 & -\sin(\pi/6) \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ \sin(\pi/6) & 0 & \cos(\pi/6) \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} \cos(\pi/3) & 0 & -\sin(\pi/3) \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ \sin(\pi/3) & 0 & \cos(\pi/3) \end{pmatrix} \\
&= \begin{pmatrix} \cos(\pi/2) & 0 & -\sin(\pi/2) \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ \sin(\pi/2) & 0 & \cos(\pi/2) \end{pmatrix},
\end{align*}
where $\cos(\pi/2) = 0$, $\sin(\pi/2) = 1$.

As $\mathbf{B}^3$ cycles through $2\pi$, then $\mathbf{B}^{1440} = (\mathbf{B}^3)^{480} = \mathbf{I}$, the identity matrix.

\[
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix}}
\]

